{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EbVfWccBYR9BuyAnMpmMn6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully before proposing tasks.\n\n## Step 1 & 2: Analyze Project State\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. The goal is to derive formal specifications from C source code and generate Go implementations, verifying parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementation of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State:**\nLooking at the implementation status, the core packages and utilities have reached specification completion with code at the generation-merged tags: testutils, sys, and format are all at their respective spec versions, while the command implementations (ts, wc, cat) are similarly spec-complete with code merged.\n\nThe remaining commands show varying progress—sponge is spec-complete, but ls and du are still in progress on their specifications, with PRDs done but use cases and test suites still needed.\n\nThere's a discrepancy I'm noticing: the architecture indicates code exists at generation-merged tags, but the analysis section shows all use cases as \"not started\" with zero test files on the working branch, and the current release is rel01.0 still in progress.\n\nFor rel01.0, the priority is clear—I need to focus on the ts specification and differential test work, since rel01.0-uc001-ts shows the spec is in progress but code hasn't started yet. The blocker is that pkg/testutils needs to exist as a dependency before cmd/ts can be implemented, so I should start by generating the code for pkg first.\n\nGiven the constraint of proposing just one task, implementing pkg/testutils (prd001) is the logical first step since it unblocks the ts utility work that follows.\n\nThe implementation would cover the core DiffTest struct, binary execution capabilities, comparison and reporting logic, normalization hooks like TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison functionality. Following the naming convention from the PRD, I'd structure this with difftest.go as the primary file since DiffTest is the main exported type.\n\nI'm realizing there's a tension here—the guidelines say to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but I'm constrained to propose just one. Since implementation must come before tests, I should focus the single task on the core types and RunDiffTests function, keeping it within the 150-250 line budget and leaving the test suite for a follow-up task. task:\n\n**Task: Implement pkg/testutils -- DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests (prd001 R1-R5)**\n\nFiles:\n- `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` - create\n  - DiffTest struct (R1)\n  - RunDiffTests function (R2, R3, R4, R5)\n  - TimestampNormalizer (R4.2)\n\nLet me map out the scope here. The PRD requirements break down into five main areas: the DiffTest struct itself with its fields and types, binary execution with dual paths and output capture, comparison and reporting logic, normalization hooks including a timestamp normalizer, and file-state comparison. I'm estimating around 195 lines total across the struct definition, the Normalize function type, the main RunDiffTests function, the TimestampNormalizer implementation, any file expectation types, and supporting helper functions—which fits comfortably within the 150-250 line budget. I'm realizing this should be a single implementation task rather than trying to cover everything at once, since the planning constitution calls for splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks. Let me review the requirements again and structure the task description properly with the required sections: deliverable type, reading materials, files, requirements anchored to the PRD, design decisions, and acceptance criteria.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the /testutils implementation against the PRD items—the DiffTest struct with its fields, the RunDiffTests function that executes both binaries with matching inputs, the comparison logic for stdout, stderr, and exit codes, the Normalize function composition pattern, the TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison.\n\nI've settled on difftest.go as the primary file, RunDiffTests taking a testing.T pointer along with the binary paths and test slice, a 10-second context timeout, Normalize as a slice to support composition, and FileExpectation to hold the path and expected content. The acceptance criteria cover the DiffTest struct fields, RunDiffTests behavior, and the comparison outputs. iffTests runs both binaries with identical stdin/args/env and compares stdout/stderr/exit code\n- AC3: Divergence report includes args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), both stdouts, both stderrs, both exit codes\n- AC4: TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps with fixed placeholder\n- AC5: Test timeout causes failure with clear timeout message rather than hanging\n- AC6: File-state comparison correctly fails when Go binary writes different content than reference binary\n- AC7: Code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint\n\nThat's 7 acceptance criteria - within range.\n\nNow I'm laying out the YAML task structure with the deliverable type, required reading materials, and the files to create. I need to define the DiffTest struct with its fields for arguments, stdin, environment variables, exit codes, normalizers, and file expectations, along with the supporting types like Normalize and FileExpectation.\n\nThe RunDiffTests function needs to execute both binaries with matching parameters, apply a 10-second timeout per invocation, and run each test as a named subtest. For comparison, I'll apply normalizations to both outputs then do byte-for-byte comparison of stdout, stderr, and exit codes. When there's a divergence, the report should include the test arguments, truncated stdin, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n\nI also need to implement the Normalize function type and the TimestampNormalizer that replaces timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder. Looking at the file structure and API design, the primary implementation goes in pkg/testutils/difftest.go following the project's naming conventions. The RunDiffTests function takes a testing.T, two binary paths, and a slice of test cases, with each test running in its own temporary directory. I'm setting a 10-second timeout per binary invocation using context, and when a timeout occurs the test fails with an appropriate message.\n\nThe Normalize field is a slice of functions that get applied sequentially to both binaries' output, and FileExpectation lets me verify that specific files exist with expected content after the binaries finish running. The DiffTest struct needs to include Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, and the Normalize slice to meet the acceptance criteria. Normalize, FileExpectations []FileExpectation.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests executes both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, Env, and working directory. Both produce independently captured stdout, stderr, and exit code.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"On stdout divergence, the failure message includes the DiffTest args, the first 256 bytes of stdin, the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, both stderrs, and both exit codes.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer applied to 'Feb 19 12:34:56 hello' replaces the timestamp prefix with 'TIMESTAMP', producing 'TIMESTAMP hello'.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A DiffTest whose binary invocation exceeds 10 seconds fails with a clear timeout message and doesn't hang the test run.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a FileExpectation whose Content differs from the actual file written by the Go binary causes RunDiffTests to report a failure with both expected and actual file contents.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"pkg/testutils compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint (go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no errors).\"\n\nI'm estimating the scope here—the main logic lives in difftest.go with the core structures and RunDiffTests function taking up most of the space, plus supporting types and imports. This should fit comfortably within the 150-250 line target. I'm checking the project context to verify whether pkg/testutils already has implementation—the spec is marked complete at rel01.0 but the code exists only at generation-merged tags, not on the current working branch, so I should propose implementing it as a new task. I also need to make sure I'm not duplicating any existing issues before finalizing. I'm ready to write out the final response now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72761,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72761},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef65ad81-a360-4e45-86c4-667810fd0c85","uuid":"6fde3d49-7ae7-49e0-a5a6-dbc98c66e5bb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EbVfWccBYR9BuyAnMpmMn6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 2: Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven pipeline that derives formal specifications from C source (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils), generates idiomatic Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies correctness through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system, not to ship production utilities.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (runs Go binary and GNU reference binary side-by-side, compares stdout/stderr/exit code)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions (terminal width, extended file metadata, signal handling)\n- `pkg/format/` — shared output formatting (column alignment, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `cmd/<utility>/` — one Go package per utility, grows with the roadmap\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build targets; delegates to mage-claude-orchestrator for code generation\n- `docs/` — PRDs, use cases, test suites, vision, architecture\n\n**Current State:** All PRDs and use cases are written. No generated Go code exists on the working branch (`generation-2026-02-26-21-54-45`); previous generated code lives at generation-merged tags only. The `analysis.code_status` confirms all use cases are `codestatus: not started` with `testfiles: 0`.\n\n**Current Release:** `rel01.0` (in_progress) — Batch 1: ts specification and differential test. The single use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` is `codestatus: not started`. `rel01.1` and later are pending.\n\n## Step 3: Priority Reasoning\n\nDependencies for `rel01.0-uc001-ts`:\n1. `pkg/testutils` must exist first — `cmd/ts` differential tests call `RunDiffTests` from this package (prd001 is a prerequisite of every downstream use case)\n2. `cmd/ts` implementation comes after the harness exists\n3. `cmd/ts` tests come after implementation\n\nThe single task for this session: implement `pkg/testutils` (prd001 R1–R5). This is the foundational library that every utility's differential tests depend on.\n\n## Step 4 & 5: Proposed Task\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils -- DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component section and design decision 2)\n      - go.mod (module name for import paths)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct, Normalize type, FileExpectation struct, RunDiffTests function, TimestampNormalizer\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement the DiffTest struct per prd001 R1.1-R1.4 with fields: Args []string, Stdin []byte (nil means EOF immediately), Env []string (nil means inherit the test process environment), ExitCode int, Normalize []Normalize, and FileExpectations []FileExpectation.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) per prd001 R2.1-R2.5. Run each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run). Execute both binaries with identical Args, Stdin, Env, and a per-test working directory created by t.TempDir(). Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently. Enforce a 10-second default timeout per binary invocation via context.WithTimeout.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison and divergence reporting per prd001 R3.1-R3.6. Apply each Normalize function in the slice to both binaries stdout and stderr in order, then compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and exit codes exactly. On any divergence call t.Errorf with: Args, Stdin truncated to 256 bytes, reference stdout, Go stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, both exit codes.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement the Normalize function type (func([]byte) []byte) per prd001 R4.1 and R4.3. The Normalize field on DiffTest is a slice. When the slice is nil or empty, no transformation is applied. RunDiffTests applies each function in the slice in sequence, passing the output of one as input to the next.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer per prd001 R4.2. It must replace strftime-format timestamp patterns matching the ts default format (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with the fixed placeholder string 'TIMESTAMP'. Use a compiled regexp that covers month abbreviations, day, and HH:MM:SS.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison per prd001 R5.1-R5.2. Add FileExpectation struct with fields Path string (relative to working directory) and Content []byte. After both binaries exit, for each FileExpectation in the test case, read the actual file from the Go binary's working directory and compare its content byte-for-byte against Content. Report divergence with expected and actual file bytes in the same style as stdout divergence.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary source file is pkg/testutils/difftest.go, not testutils.go. The primary exported type is DiffTest so the file is named difftest.go per the project file naming convention (testutils/testutils.go is explicitly forbidden by the project constitution).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"The Normalize field on DiffTest is []Normalize (a slice), not a single function, per prd001 R4.3. RunDiffTests composes the slice by applying each function in sequence to both binaries stdout and stderr independently.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Timeout is enforced per prd001 R2.3 via context.WithTimeout with a fixed 10-second default. Each binary runs in its own goroutine; if exec.CommandContext is cancelled by the deadline, the test fails with a message naming the binary and the test case. Do not make the timeout configurable -- the PRD fixes it at 10 seconds.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"FileExpectation.Path is relative to the working directory of the binary invocation (t.TempDir() per test). RunDiffTests reads the file from the Go binary working directory for comparison. Reference binary working directory is separate; its file output is not checked against FileExpectation.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Do not emit any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing run per prd001 R2.4. All reporting goes through t.Errorf or t.Fatalf so that go test output is clean on success.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct has all required fields (Args []string, Stdin []byte, Env []string, ExitCode int, Normalize []Normalize, FileExpectations []FileExpectation) and compiles without error.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) executes both binaries with identical Args, Stdin, and Env and captures stdout, stderr, and exit code from each independently.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"On stdout divergence, the failure message includes the DiffTest Args, the first 256 bytes of Stdin, the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, both stderrs, and both exit codes.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer('Feb 19 12:34:56 hello') returns a string where the timestamp prefix is replaced with 'TIMESTAMP', producing output that contains 'TIMESTAMP hello'.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A binary invocation that exceeds 10 seconds causes the subtest to fail with a clear timeout message and does not block the test run indefinitely.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a FileExpectation whose Content differs from the file the Go binary writes causes RunDiffTests to call t.Errorf with both the expected and actual file contents.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no errors on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72761,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72761},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef65ad81-a360-4e45-86c4-667810fd0c85","uuid":"25b9fa10-e837-459d-99bd-f29cef80d6dd"}
